    "question": "Jim collects model cars, and he has 301 models total.  Jim has 4 times as many Buicks as Fords, and 3 more than twice the number of Fords than Chevys.  How many Buicks does Jim have?",
    "gold": "220",
    "answer": "Let x represent the number of Chevys\nFords:3+2x\nBuicks:4(3+2x)=12+8x\nTotal:x+3+2x+12+8x=301\n11x+15=301\n11x=286\nx=<<26=26>>26\nBuicks:12+8(26)=220\n#### 220"
  },
  "generation": {
    "text": "To determine the number of Buicks Jim has, we need to set up and solve a system of equations based on the information given. Let's define the variables as follows:\n- Let \\( C \\) be the number of Chevys.\n- Let \\( F \\) be the number of Fords.\n- Let \\( B \\) be the number of Buicks.\n\nFrom the problem, we know the following:\n1. Jim has 301 model cars in total.\n2. Jim has 4 times as many Buicks as Fords.\n3. Jim has 3 more than twice the number of Fords than Chevys.\n\nWe can translate these statements into equations:\n1. \\( C + F + B = 301 \\)\n2. \\( B = 4F \\)\n3. \\( F = 2C + 3 \\)\n\nFirst, we substitute the expression for \\( F \\) from the third equation into the second equation:\n\\[ B = 4(2C + 3) = 8C + 12 \\]\n\nNow we have expressions for \\( B \\) and \\( F \\) in terms of \\( C \\):\n\\[ B = 8C + 12 \\]\n\\[ F = 2C + 3 \\]\n\nNext, we substitute these expressions into the first equation:\n\\[ C + (2C + 3) + (8C + 12) = 301 \\]\n\\[ C + 2C + 3 + 8C + 12 = 301 \\]\n\\[ 11C + 15 = 301 \\]\n\\[ 11C = 286 \\]\n\\[ C = 26 \\]\n\nNow that we have \\( C = 26 \\), we can find \\( F \\) and \\( B \\):\n\\[ F = 2C + 3 = 2(26) + 3 = 52 + 3 = 55 \\]\n\\[ B = 4F = 4(55) = 220 \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of Buicks Jim has is \\(\\boxed{220}\\).",
